WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.668
With the rise of cyberattacks and data breaches,

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:07.889
it's never been more important to make sure that your organization is protected against hackers.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.070
This series is about cybersecurity architecture, and we're going to talk about two different areas.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:20.160
Fundamentals, where we're going to go through and discover what some of the principles of cybersecurity are

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.859
that need to be applied to everything that you do.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:27.030
And then the second part is on various cybersecurity domains.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:33.379
Here we're going to explore how to identify vulnerabilities, implement best practices,

00:00:33.379 --> 00:00:40.259
and defend against a wide range of cyber threats through an all encompassing cybersecurity architecture.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:49.050
By the way, I'm an adjunct professor at NC State University.
And there I teach a 400 level course on enterprise security architecture.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.560
This video series is based upon that course.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.969
The bad news is you won't get college credit for watching these videos.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.750
The good news is no homework and no exams.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:01.900
So yay.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.219
All right, let's get started with cybersecurity fundamentals.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:12.909
All right, we want to start with five security principles that you absolutely should do and one that you never should do.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.400
So stay tuned to the end to find out what that one is.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:19.029
The first one we're going to talk about is this notion of "defense in depth".

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:24.279
Defense in depth is trying to create an obstacle course, a difficulty for the bad guy.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:28.984
So if we take a look at an old security model, the castle.

00:01:28.984 --> 00:01:34.810
Well, the castle was designed with thick, tall walls to keep the good guys on the inside, the bad guys on the outside.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.159
And it worked pretty well until you realized the good guys sometimes needed to come out.

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.219
And therefore, we needed to put a door on this thing.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.090
Well, the door then became a vulnerability.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.650
And so we we would try to reinforce it.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:53.230
And then maybe put a moat around the whole thing, because that made it even harder.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.689
And then the door with a drawbridge.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:59.560
So now we've got a moat, which is harder to cross.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.150
We've got the thick, tall walls.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.925
And maybe we even had in an angry dog here on this side.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:08.009
Something to give a system of security mechanisms,

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:14.830
because defense in depth is all about not relying on any single security mechanism to keep the system safe.

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.759
Now, let's move and transition into a modern security example.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:25.580
Here, we've got a user who is on a workstation who's going to go across a network to get to a web server,

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:29.349
which is going to hit an app server, which is ultimately going to hit a database.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:33.309
Now, what would we do for defense in depth in this example?

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:36.886
Well, one thing I might do here is I might add multifactor authentication (MFA).

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:44.285
That is a system where I make sure that this user is who they are because I'm asking them for something they have,

00:02:44.285 --> 00:02:48.370
something they are, something they know--some combination of those kinds of things.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.620
Now, how about over here?

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:57.904
I might do if it's a mobile device or an endpoint of some sort--mobile device management (MDM), endpoint device management software

00:02:57.904 --> 00:03:03.430
that makes sure that the security policy that we have set for the organization is in fact followed

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:08.770
on this device-- it's got the right patches, it's got a password that's of sufficient length and things like that.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:14.139
We might also add something like an EDR, which is sort of a next generation antivirus.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:19.059
An endpoint detection and response capability to make sure that this platform is secure.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:26.619
Then from a network standpoint, well, I'm going to add in firewalls to keep the web server secure from the outside

00:03:26.619 --> 00:03:33.370
and also allow only traffic that I choose to allow to get back to these more sensitive zones.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:37.596
And then for the app server and the web server, I might do some testing,

00:03:37.596 --> 00:03:43.569
some vulnerability testing on those, so that I make sure that those systems are not vulnerable to attack.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.190
And then ultimately, I'm going to take the data back here and I'm going to encrypt it.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:51.460
Lock it up, put access controls around it.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:57.039
So you can see what I've done here, is there's no single security mechanism that is protecting this thing.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:00.819
If any one of these fails, the rest of the system still works.

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:03.099
And that's the idea that we're after here.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:08.469
So if you think about it this way: we've got no single point of failure.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:11.050
We're trying to avoid single points of failure.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:15.009
And we want a system that ultimately, if it fails, it fails safe.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.750
That's what we're trying to get.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:20.620
And that's what the old model and the new model of security were designed to do.

00:04:21.779 --> 00:04:25.211
The second principle we're going to review is the "principle of least privilege".

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:31.988
Principle of least privilege basically says I'm only going to give access rights to people that need that--

00:04:31.988 --> 00:04:38.699
that are authorized and needed in order to do their job and can justify it, and for only as long as they need that access right.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.269
For instance, in this example, I've got three users.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:46.920
This guy does not really have a business need, so we don't give it to him.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.478
The other guys get the access right.

00:04:48.478 --> 00:04:49.980
They can prove their need.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:53.639
And the other thing is, even for these guys, the clock is ticking.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:57.299
I'm not going to give them this access right in perpetuity, forever.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:03.180
We're going to constantly be going back and making sure that they still need that capability.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.790
If they don't, we're going to remove it from them as well.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:10.320
Now, another notion in the principle of least privilege is hardening a system.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.346
Let's say we've got a web server like this and the web server out of the box--

00:05:14.346 --> 00:05:20.040
default configuration--is that it runs a HTTP, of course, because we need that in order to do web traffic.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:23.790
But let's say it also turns on an FTP service

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:27.540
and SSH service so that I can log in remotely.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:32.949
Well, there's some things that I might look at and say, "Okay, do I really need this FTP server?"

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:36.990
If it turns out I'm not going to use it, I should remove that service entirely.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:41.035
In the SSH, if I'm not planning to use it, remove it entirely.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:48.689
Because every single one of these services is potentially expanding our attack surface and making us more vulnerable.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:54.262
Another example of hardening is to remove all of the unnecessary IDs

00:05:54.262 --> 00:05:59.160
that are on the system and change the names of the IDs that we do keep from their default.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:06.749
So, for instance, if the administrator ID on this system--out of the box as it's configured--is admin, let's change that.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.929
Let's make it something more specific.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.050
And I'll name it after me, or give it some other name.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:14.879
Change all the default passwords.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:21.660
We don't want this thing in just a vanilla configuration because the bad guys will know what that is and they'll know how to break in.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:25.350
Now another example is this idea of privilege creep.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:26.430
Let me illustrate that.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:32.040
Let's say there's two people that work for the company, and each one of these are access rights that they have.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.410
So this guy is able to do these things.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:37.470
He can do the same things because they perform essentially the same role.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:42.810
Now, this guy gets a promotion and a new job and new responsibilities.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:46.649
Well, he goes to the administrator and says, "Okay, now I'm doing my new job.

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.319
I need you to add to my capabilities.

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:54.869
And these are the things I need." The administrator gives him those and then also says, "You know what?

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:58.319
Just in case, I think you're going to probably need this.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:06:59.700
Let me give you that as well.

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:01.878
That way you won't have to come back and ask again."

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.500
Or, come back and bother me, is what he really means.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:10.259
The problem with this is, just-in-case is just the opposite of principle of least privilege.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:15.359
In fact, what we should be doing is running an annual recertification campaign.

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:17.440
At least annual.

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:19.690
Some organizations do it more frequently than that.

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:26.410
And in re-cert, I go back and look at all of my users and all of their access rights and make sure they still have a justified need.

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:29.739
So this person is still doing the same job, still needs all of that.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:30.790
Great, they keep it.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:35.499
This guy, though, no longer needs this capability because his new job doesn't require it.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:36.699
So we're taking it away.

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:40.750
And this thing that he got just in case, we're taking that away, too.

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:47.740
So what we're trying to do with the principle of least privilege is to give only the access rights you need for as long as you need them.

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:49.810
Hardened systems.

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:55.195
We're going to eliminate privilege creep, and we're going to eliminate the just-in-case principle.

00:07:56.110 --> 00:08:02.019
Our third principle to keep in mind with cybersecurity is this notion of separation of duties.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:07.329
That is, we won't have any single point of control.

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:15.669
In fact, what we're trying to do is force collusion by two bad actors-- or more than two bad actors --in order to compromise the system.

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:18.550
But no single person can create the compromise.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:25.480
So, an example, in the physical world, would be if I had two people here and I've got a door with two locks on it.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:31.630
And this guy has a key to this lock and this guy has a key to this lock.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:38.379
And what it is, is now, if he uses his key to open the door, he still can't open the door.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.119
He can't open the door alone.

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:44.890
But the two of them together, cooperating, can in fact, open the door.

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:47.650
So there's no single point of control.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:50.729
Therefore, we have a separation of duties.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:56.740
Now, taking a look at another example here, let's say in an IT case, here's a requester.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:59.409
And this user wants access to this database.

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:00.990
So he's going to ask for that.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:02.860
He's going to send in his request.

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:09.309
But then there's an approver who's going to have to take action on it and say yes or no based upon whether we think they should have it or not.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:14.140
Then if they get the approval, then they're given the action that they want.

00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:20.649
Whatever it is, the funds transfer, the access to the database, the package delivered, whatever it happens to be.

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:22.239
But notice the point here.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:26.859
This person, the requester, is not the same as the approver.

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.230
They cannot be the same person.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:34.869
Because if it was, if I could request and approve my own request, then there is no separation of duties.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:40.087
So again, what we're trying to do with this is create a necessary case for collusion,

00:09:40.087 --> 00:09:45.610
which is hard to do because it's hard for lots of people to work together and keep a good secret.

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:50.873
And what we're trying to avoid is this single point of control.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:55.980
The fourth security principle that we're going to talk about is secure by design.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.820
In other words, it shouldn't be an afterthought that we put security in.

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:06.929
Think of it this way: If you were designing a building in an earthquake zone, you want to make this building able to stand the pressure.

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:13.306
So, you don't go build the building, and then after it's all done, go back and say, "Now let's make it earthquake-proof".

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:20.099
No, you want to do that from start-to-finish, all the way from design through completion.

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:22.350
So let's take a look at an IT example.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:27.149
So when we have a project, we will tend to start off with requirements stage.

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:31.352
We'll go then into design, we will code the thing,

00:10:31.352 --> 00:10:37.649
then we'll install whatever it is that we've written, then we'll test it out and then we'll promote it to production.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:44.639
And then, in theory, we should feed that loop back and continue the continuous development process that way.

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:49.762
Well, what we don't want to do, is what too many people do in these cases,

00:10:49.762 --> 00:10:55.559
and they wait until really about this phase to do security--once it's already out there.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:58.710
Security can't just be a bolt-on that you do at the end.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:02.940
In fact, it needs to be something that we're doing throughout, pervasively.

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:05.700
We look at the security aspects of the requirements.

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:08.070
We build security into the design.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:12.629
We are thinking about secure coding principles all along the path.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.779
When we install, we do it on a secure system.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:19.290
We're testing and and guarding that test data.

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:21.509
And then in production, obviously, we keep testing.

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:26.490
So security is something we do throughout, but it doesn't begin here.

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:28.169
It begins in these phases.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:30.030
That's what we're really looking for here.

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:35.620
Now, if you think about another example, let's say: Whose job is security?

00:11:35.830 --> 00:11:36.940
Well, it's really everyone here.

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:39.369
We have a designer, an administrator and a user.

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:44.380
So really, all of them are responsible for security in one way or another.

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:46.929
But who does the job begin with?

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:49.540
Well, it begins with this guy right here.

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:52.960
We need to make sure that he is designing security in.

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:57.159
In other words, what we're trying to do is make security start to finish.

00:11:57.610 --> 00:12:01.599
And we want "secure by design", means it's secure out of the box.

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:03.309
That's the way we'd like it to be.

00:12:03.340 --> 00:12:07.239
Now, sometimes we're going to have to do some configuration changes to make it more secure.

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:13.305
But this is the goal that we're trying to shoot for--secure by design, secure out of the box.

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:18.299
Our fifth security principle is the "K.I.S.S. principle".

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:20.841
It stands for "Keep It Simple, Stupid".

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:28.499
In other words, we don't want to make it harder than necessary because that will make it easier for the bad guys and harder for the good guys.

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:35.220
To give you an example: we're trying to create some level of complexity so that it's not easy for the bad guy to get in.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:42.509
But a lot of times the security department will create this complex maze of things that the good guys essentially have to go through.

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:47.399
And what happens in that case is, I start in here, okay, I log in.

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:52.860
Now, I have to traverse and eventually I'm like, "Oh, I'm at a dead end".

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:54.570
Okay, maybe let's try this again.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:55.890
You know what?

00:12:55.890 --> 00:12:59.519
It's too much trouble to do what the security department has asked me to do.

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:05.789
I'm just going to subvert this, and I'm going to end up doing it that way, which is, of course, not what we're after.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:13.740
So the lesson here is, if we make it harder to do the right thing than it is to do the wrong thing, people are going to do the wrong thing.

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:18.730
So we need to be able to make the system secure, but also as simple as possible.

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:20.632
So keep it simple, stupid.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:24.450
Here's an example of how we do this in security departments, for real.

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:26.549
We'll come up with password rules.

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:31.169
So we'll say this is your password and it equals this.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:36.982
And it's this because we created a complex set of rules that say you have to start with an uppercase,

00:13:36.982 --> 00:13:42.342
then you follow with a lowercase, then you need a special character, then you need to throw in some numbers,

00:13:42.342 --> 00:13:48.270
and then you have to have some mixture of upper and lower case and special characters and all this kind of stuff. And it has to be really long.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:50.730
And by the way, we need lots of these.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:55.049
You're going to have a different one on every system, and I'm going to make you change it on a frequent basis.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:14:02.099
That's this-- that's what the user sees --is a complex maze, and they're going to find a way to do this.

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:05.713
And what they're going to do is find one password and write it down

00:14:05.713 --> 00:14:10.290
and set all their systems equal to the same thing, which is again, not what we were after.

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:16.949
So what we want to do is understand complexity is the enemy of security.

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:21.144
So we want to make the system just complex enough to keep the bad guys out,

00:14:21.144 --> 00:14:25.020
but not so complex that it's hard for the good guys to do what they need to do.

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:30.049
For instance, you might have noticed, well, what about Defense in Depth, which I talked about up here?

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:33.141
There might be some conflict in that because there we're trying to set

00:14:33.141 --> 00:14:38.010
a system of security mechanisms in place to put an obstacle course for the bad guy.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:42.179
We want that obstacle course to be for the bad guy, not for the good guy.

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:48.750
All right, now we've gone over five security principles that you should always observe.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:55.470
And now the big reveal, the security principle you should never observe, and that is security by obscurity.

00:14:55.920 --> 00:15:00.629
That is, relying on some sort of secret knowledge in order to make the system safe.

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:05.639
It turns out that secrecy and security are not the same thing.

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:10.590
In fact, what we want is a system that is open and observable.

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:17.343
And this guy called Kerckhoff came up with what's now known as Kerckhoff's Principle, which basically describes that.

00:15:17.343 --> 00:15:20.460
He was specifically talking about a crypto system.

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:27.000
And he said basically, a crypto system should be secure if you know every single thing about it except for the key.

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:30.390
In other words, the key is the only secret in the whole system.

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:32.609
Now, why would this be an issue?

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:38.469
Well, it turns out a lot of people and you should whenever you hear this, you should run and not walk,

00:15:38.469 --> 00:15:45.886
but run away when you hear somebody say, "I've invented a crypto system that's proprietary and it will take your clear text,

00:15:45.886 --> 00:15:53.248
you feed it into my algorithm along with a key, and then it will produce from clear text to ciphertext".

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:54.790
Okay, great.

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:56.879
The problem is this is a black box.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:58.799
I can't see how it's working.

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:06.510
And if the individual says it's unbreakable, I've been hacking at it for weeks, months, years.

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:10.307
All that means is the inventor couldn't find a way to break it.

00:16:10.323 --> 00:16:15.619
But that's not the same thing as the whole world, given access, would, in fact, break this.

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:17.459
And, in fact, given time, they will.

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:19.109
Even if it is a black box.

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:21.000
History has shown that to be the case.

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:26.970
So what we want is not black box security, we want glass box security.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:35.309
So in this case, what we have is the clear text, goes into a crypto algorithm, which we understand, it's been published.

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:45.394
In fact, if you look at the good crypto algorithms that we rely on today, it's things like, AES, the Advanced Encryption Standard and RSA.

00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:51.239
These are algorithms that everyone that wants to know how they work can look it up and see.

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:57.029
And the secrecy, therefore, the security doesn't come from some secret knowledge of how this thing works.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:03.929
It's able to produce ciphertext from clear text without having to keep this part secret.

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:06.209
The only secret is the key.

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:07.649
And that's what we want.

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:15.789
We do the same kind of things when we talk about secure operating systems or secure applications or things like that.

00:17:16.060 --> 00:17:21.670
As long as the security is based on secrecy, it's not really something that we can rely on.

00:17:22.736 --> 00:17:23.794
Thanks for watching!

00:17:23.794 --> 00:17:30.856
Before you leave, don't forget to hit subscribe. That way you won't miss the next installment of the cybersecurity architecture series.

